Dock cards and the move gestures.

- The `move` gesture, started on a dock pile card, drives the pile live along the axis
  toward the dock edge, in the PHYSICAL frame: the dock sits on the panel's physical
  right edge, so a swipe toward that edge means the same on every rotation. Touchpad
  deltas are physical already; touchscreen ones (CTrackpadGestures::m_fromTouchscreen,
  set by 11) are logical and get mapped through the monitor transform, the frame
  ksOrientToEdge and dock.sh's EDGE use.
    toward the edge  the front card follows the fingers out; released past 60px, the
                     card right behind it comes to the front.
    away from it     every card but the last follows, each deeper card travelling
                     gestures:dock_swipe_follow (0.75) of the one in front -- the lag;
                     released past 60px, the last card comes to the front.
    far away         past gestures:dock_swipe_pullout (0.35) of the monitor along the
                     dock axis, the front card comes OUT: it follows the fingers 1:1,
                     along the edge as well, while the trailing cards ease back to rest
                     over the last fifth before the line (no jump); released there, it
                     is undocked. Back under the line it is an ordinary "prev" again.
    far toward it    past gestures:dock_swipe_dismiss (0.35) of the monitor toward the
                     edge, the rest of the pile catches up with the front card over the
                     last fifth before the line and goes off the edge with it; released
                     there, the dock hides. Back under the line it is an ordinary "next".
  Depth comes from the `dockd<N>` tags features/sidedock's render() sets. Release runs
  `gestures:dock_swipe_exec <next|prev|out|hide|none> <front>`, which features/sidedock points at
  `dock.sh gesture-move` to cycle or spring back from where the cards were left. Before
  2026-10-08 only the touched card moved, horizontally in LOGICAL space, and left->right
  meant "previous" -- the last card, which read as backwards. One gesture rather than a
  second 3-finger one: Hyprland runs only the first gesture matching a finger count.
- The `float` gesture leaves dock windows alone (floating toggles break the pile).
- The scroll_move gesture drives the regular workspace when the open special
  workspace has no tiled windows (the dock's), so it keeps working with the dock open.

